The water tank is a cone in the shape of an inverted right circular cone. To solve this problem, we need to determine the height of the water when the tank is 25% full. We use the similarity of the smaller cone to the larger cone. The ratio of the volumes of two similar cones is the cube of the ratio of their corresponding heights. If the original cone has height \(h = 96\) feet and radius \(r = 16\) feet, and the water fills 25% of the tank, then the volume of the water forms a cone with a height \(h_w\) and radius \(r_w\). Since the volumes are proportional to the cube of the heights, we have: \[
\left(\frac{h_w}{h}\right)^3 = 0.25
\]
This gives us: \[
\left(\frac{h_w}{96}\right)^3 = \frac{1}{4}
\] Taking the cube root of both sides, we get: \[
\frac{h_w}{96} = \frac{1}{\sqrt[3]{4}}
\] Thus: \[
h_w = \frac{96}{\sqrt[3]{4}}
\]
To simplify this, we express \(96\) as \(2^5 \cdot 3\), and \(\sqrt[3]{4} = \sqrt[3]{2^2}\), so: \[
h_w = \frac{2^5 \cdot 3}{2^{2/3}} = 2^{5-2/3} \cdot 3^{1/3} = 2^{13/3} \cdot 3^{1/3}
\] This simplifies to: \[
h_w = 8\sqrt[3]{3}
\] So, \(a = 8\) and \(b = 3\), and \(a + b = 11\). </think> <answer> 11 </answer>

